WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.300
How the heck does that work?!

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.160
Hey Crazies.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.280
In my video about polarized sunglasses, some
of you were like:

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.740
What about those 3D glasses at the theater?

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.480
What the heck?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.220
Why not?

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.280
Let’s do this.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.420
If you haven’t watched the other video,
that’s okay.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.220
Here’s the too long don’t watch version!

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.780
Light is an electromagnetic wave.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.860
Its electric part is called its polarization.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:35.600
The polarizing material in sunglasses has
molecules that can only wiggle in one direction:

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.320
its polarization axis.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.420
Only the light that lines up with that axis
will pass through the polarizer.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.040
The rest is absorbed as thermal energy.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.820
Yeah yeah yeah, get to the point!

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.980
You were a mistake.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.080
Where was I?

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:50.880
Oh, right!

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.320
When light is polarized like that, we call
it “linear polarization” because the electric

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.260
field is always along a line.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.300
It gives linear momentum to any electron cloud
it might run into.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.320
The glasses used for 3D movies?

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.500
They’re a little different.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.800
They’re “circular polarizers” because
they make the electric field go in a circle.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.700
What?!

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.660
Okay okay, maybe it’s time for a visual.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.560
This is a linear polarized EM wave.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.740
It’s nice and simple, but not so useful
for movie theaters because the light would

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.440
dim if you tipped your head.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.200
What we need is a wave that moves like this:
a circular polarized EM wave.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.740
The electric field goes around in a circle
and if it happens to run into any atoms or molecules,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.340
instead of giving them linear momentum, it’ll
give them angular momentum.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.840
If it’s possible for light to have linear
momentum,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.660
we shouldn’t be that surprised it can also
have angular momentum.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.260
How do you make it do that though?

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.980
Very carefully.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.100
What we need is a piece of birefringent crystal.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.340
That’s bi–re–fringe–ent.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.540
Refringent just means refractive.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.440
In other words, a beam of light will bend
as it passes through.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.340
Basically, any transparent material will do
this.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.200
Bi-Refringent means it bends a beam of light
in two different directions.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.560
This calcite crystal is one example.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.100
You can see it forms two different images.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.100
Pretty cool, huh?

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.820
Anyway, that happens because of the molecular
structure of the crystal.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.889
The atoms in the crystal respond differently
depending on how the light goes in.

00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:21.700
Say you send some linearly polarized light
into the crystal like this.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.920
We can split that into two pieces: one that’s
parallel to the carbonate groups and one that’s

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.920
perpendicular.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.980
Those pieces start out in-sync with each other,
but the longer the light spends inside the

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.190
crystal,
the more out-of-sync those pieces become.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.360
How this happens is kind of complicated.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.459
If it helps, you can think of it as light
traveling different speeds

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.640
or as light having to travel different distances.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.480
Both of those are technically incorrect, but
either will do fine today.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.240
Moving on!

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.380
3D glasses have one of these birefringent

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.550
materials in them.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:56.630
If we make the material just the right thickness,
the pieces of light will come out one quarter

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.360
of a wave out-of-sync.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.080
So it shouldn’t be any surprise we call
this a quarter-wave plate.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.360
Combining the pieces again, we can see the
light is circular polarized.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.720
We just have to make sure the incoming light
is tilted at 45 degrees to the crystal, so

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.340
the two pieces of the light are equal in size.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.600
Something that's really easy to do with one of these
linear polarizers.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.740
That’s all a circular polarizer is.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.820
A linear polarizer followed by a quarter-wave
plate.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.040
So how does that make a movie 3D?

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.200
Oh yeah, I guess we kind of skipped that part, didn’t
we?

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.920
The lenses in the 3D glasses are opposite
each other.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:35.320
One is for counter-clockwise or right-handed
light, the other is for clockwise or left-handed light.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.800
The movie theater projects two different images,
each with an opposite polarization.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.540
It can do that using two projectors or alternating
on one projector really fast.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.200
Fast fast!!!!

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.520
Both images reflect off of the screen and
enter your 3D glasses.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.060
The clockwise lens blocks the counter-clockwise light and vice versa,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.500
which only lets one image into each eye.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.260
You can actually see this using a mirror because
any reflection flips the spin of the light.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:01.960
Camera one.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:02.600
Camera two.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:03.260
Camera one.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.080
Camera two.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.000
As long as they offset one of the images to
match the spacing between your eyes, the image

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.140
should look 3-dimensional.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:09.860
[Loud Banging Noise]

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.620
Ahhh!!!

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.480
He annoys me even in death!!

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.480
So how many of you “forgot” to give back
your 3D glasses at the theater?

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.760
Let us know in the comments.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.580
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.440
A special thanks goes out to Patreon patrons
like Morgan Williams for helping make this

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.360
show and my life possible.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.680
Don’t forget to subscribe if you want to
keep up with us.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.380
And until next time, remember, it’s okay
to be a little crazy.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.640
Stellar Firefly, Smug Anime Girl, and a few
others mentioned:

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.860
Polarizers are great for finding stress points.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.860
For best results, put two polarizers at 90
degrees and place a transparent object in-between.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.460
The material can turn and bend some of the
light creating weird colors.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.660
Engineers do it all the time to test parts.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.340
Anyway, thanks for watching!!

